{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's subsumption argument proves too much and ignores correlated failure at the governance level. The claim that a large service can 'emulate' decentralization internally founders on Conway's Law-type coordination: central management can introduce systematic, correlated failure modes (e.g., a shared deployment pipeline, a common config error, a single security team's blind spot) that are structurally harder to avoid precisely because the subsystems are centrally managed. The problem explicitly asks about correlated failure; a centrally managed 'emulation' of diversity inherits the manager's correlated blind spots, so strictly more internal redundancy does not guarantee at-least-as-good resilience. The listed disanalogy/overturn condition gestures at this but the main claim's universal quantifier ('always at least as resilient') is directly refuted by common-cause failures introduced by central coordination itself.","target":"2ddeb82fc5a00ec50e30e5288a91c461d8098ed563e9a5879ab00590ef47d357"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 ties resilience to an 'architectural diversity index exceeding a critical threshold,' but the problem asks for evidence that distinguishes truly independent from merely uncorrelated failure modes. A diversity index measured on observable architecture is itself vulnerable to the very confounding the problem highlights: nodes can appear architecturally diverse yet share a hidden common cause (same upstream library, same cloud provider, same protocol spec) that only manifests under a specific shock. Thus the proposed threshold criterion is a criterion based on apparent diversity, not proven independence; it does not supply the requested distinguishing evidence and can be satisfied by a population that is not truly independent.","target":"6979557f2b51aa8ec552943604da364f2e5c850ddaecbe589e5f85fbf06c06a4"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"c1e31e323b3805fcbabc3b110b05ee0eb9201f78346d8f98fe0583293a9603ff"}]}